The AI TAPE: A Human-Steward's Quick-Start Guide
An AI TAPE is your project's "Save Game." It captures not just your files, but the intelligence, decisions, and momentum of your conversation. Use it to move your work between different AI models, different chat sessions, or different devices without ever having to "re-explain" yourself.

🚀 1. The Core Loop: How to Work
Every productive session follows a simple three-step cycle:

LOAD: Start a new chat by pasting your latest .ai-tape.md file.
ACT: Do your work. Solve puzzles, write code, or brainstorm ideas.
SAVE: Before you close the chat, ask the AI to create a new "Checkpoint Tape."
🛠️ 2. How to Request Tasks (The Prompting Guide)
The AI is trained to respond to specific "Command Patterns." Use these to get exactly what you need.

A. Starting a Session (Loading)
When to use: Every time you start a new chat or move to a different AI model.

The Prompt: [Paste your .ai-tape.md content here] $\rightarrow$ "Load TAPE."
What happens: The AI will perform "Soft Depth," identify the project state, and tell you it is ready to work.
B. Saving Your Progress (Checkpointing)
When to use: After a productive burst, a successful code implementation, or before closing a session.

The Prompt: "Create a checkpoint tape."
What happens: The AI will synthesize everything you just did into a new, compressed, high-density tape.
Cruest Step: Download/Copy the resulting text immediately! The chat is temporary; the tape is permanent.
C. Deep-Dive / High-Resolution (Rich Restoration)
When to use: When you are moving to a much more powerful AI (e.g., moving from a local model to GPT-4o) and you want it to have the full reasoning, history, and rationale of the project.

The Prompt: "Perform a Rich Restoration. Use the full history and all reasoning."
What happens: The AI will generate a much larger, more detailed tape that includes all the "why" and the "how" of your project.
D. Cleaning Up (Rebasing)
When to use: After a long session where the chat has become cluttered and "heavy."

The Prompt: "Rebase this session into a Master tape."
What happens: The AI will strip away the "chatter" and the "junk," leaving you with a clean, high-density Master file ready for long-term storage.
🌊 3. Controlling "Depth" (Data & Context)
You can control how much "weight" the AI carries by how you ask for it.

If you want...	Use this wording...	Why?
Speed & Low Token Use	"Create a lightweight checkpoint."	Best for quick daily saves and low-power models.
Full Intelligence	"Perform a Rich Restoration."	Best for moving to a powerful model to do heavy reasoning.
The Full History	"Include the full Chronicle."	Best when you need the AI to know exactly how you reached a decision.
Just the Files	"Create a tape with only assets."	Best when you only care about the code/data, not the conversation.
⚠️ 4. The Golden Rules of Stewardship
Don't Trust the Chat: The chat window is a "leaky bucket." Always save your tapes to your own computer.
The "Save-Ready" Check: When the AI gives you a tape, ensure it is a single, clean Markdown block. If it's broken, say: "The tape is fragmented. Provide a single, clean, save-ready Markdown block."
Don't Fear the "Rich" Tape: A larger tape is not a "bad" tape. If the project is complex, a large tape is a successful tape.
Watch for the "Summary Trap": If the AI gives you a tape that looks too "thin" (e.g., it forgets your recent code or decisions), say: "This tape is under-dense. Use the Restoration Sufficiency Check and provide a richer version."
🆘 5. Troubleshooting
"The AI forgot my project!" $\rightarrow$ You likely started a new chat without loading a tape. Paste your last .ai-tape.md and say "Load TAPE".
"The AI is acting 'stupid' or forgetful!" $\rightarrow$ Your context is likely cluttered. Create a "Master" tape to clean it up, then start a fresh chat with that Master.
"The tape is too big to paste!" $\rightarrow$ Use a "Lightweight Checkpoint" to reduce the weight, or split the work into different sub-projects.
Happy Stewardship. The continuity is in your hands.

